[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparison alone would leave them unverified. Both are now pinned:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **The `malloc` request size**, including whether
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `numLines * sizeof(const char**)` wraps. A non-`NULL` result requires
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `numLines <= bufferSize` (each loop iteration advances `pos` by at least 1),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and `bufferSize` is bounded by a real allocation, so on every *reachable*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    success path the product is small and cannot wrap. Rows E7–E9 therefore
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    cannot distinguish `wrapping_mul` from `saturating_mul` by return value —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    both end in `NULL`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `tests/support/malloc_trace.c` interposes `malloc`/`free` via `LD_PRELOAD`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and `tests/differential.rs::interpose_malloc_size_and_free_parity` asserts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the C and Rust request byte-identical sizes (and that the size equals
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `numLines.wrapping_mul(8)`, so a matched-pair regression also fails).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    Run with `./run_with_interpose.sh`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Whether the error path calls `free`.** A leak is not visible to a caller.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `interpose_error_path_frees` asserts free-call parity across all three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    outcomes: success (0 frees), buffer-exhausted error (exactly 1 free), and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `malloc`-failure error (0 frees, nothing was allocated).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **Reads past `bufferSize`.** `guard_page_no_read_past_buffer_size` places the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    buffer so its last byte abuts a `PROT_NONE` page; any over-read faults. This
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    pins the inner loop's `pos + len < bufferSize` bound, which is otherwise
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    unobservable (overshooting always leaves `pos >= bufferSize`, which exits the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    outer loop with the same `lineIndex` and the same recorded offsets).
